I think what's needed is to have two separate numbers maintained for each piece
of armor:
   a) what the base value of this armor is. This is always > 0. If this ever
falls to 0, the armor disappears into rusted iron flakes.
   b) what the magical augmentation is. This can be anything (within armor
enchantment limits).

For example, suppose we have a splint mail +2. This has a BAC (Base Armor
Class) of 6, and a ACMA (Armor Class Magical Adjustment) of +2, resulting in a
real AC Value of 8 and an real AC of 2.
   Now, suppose that we find an enchant armor scroll. Unbeknownst to us,
however, it's cursed. We read it and suddenly the ACMA goes to +1, ACV to 7, AC
to 3.
   Now (we're not having a good day here), we get hit by lots of rust (from
whatever source). We wind up rusting 5 separate times. We now have a BAC of 1,
ACV of 2, AC of 8.
   We're getting desperate, so we start reading enchant armor scrolls. This
game being what it is, all the scrolls are cursed. We lose another 3 magical
points. Now we've got an ACMA of -2, ACV of -1, AC of 11.
   For whatever reason, the armor's cursed, so we can't get rid of it. We're
really in bad shape now, so we go for another rust point (we should be able to
get this from somewhere). This causes the BAC to go to 0.
   Consequently, the armor rusts into oblivion and falls off. We're now
unarmored.
   I'm not sure about how to display the current stats of any/every piece of
armor, based on identified, un-identified status, although I have some ideas.
[Unidentified armor shows BAC, identified armor shows BAC and ACMA.]

   So, does this sound like it's do-able? It would certainly fix all of the
problems/situations that have been described so far.

